+# inv-002: F5 what-if still precedes the prod approval
+## Question
+Why does the reviewer FAIL build/F5 at attempt 2 (A2.4), and is each cause in the infrastructure, the checker, or the orchestrator's briefs?
+## Hypothesis
+The orchestrator's fix brief for the QA what-if defect specified "two jobs in one stage", on a wrong model of
+Azure DevOps (environment checks are evaluated before a stage starts, for every resource it uses); the builder
+followed the brief and the red checker encoded the same model, so no gate could catch it. Disproved if approvals
+are evaluated per job, if the builder deviated from the brief, or if the brief asked for a separate stage.
+## Evidence
+- docs/trace/build/F5/verdicts/A2.4-reviewer.json: FAIL, P1 R1 (approval still precedes the what-if), P1 R2 (checker asserts job-level separation only), P2 R3 (QA line references stale)
+- docs/trace/build/F5/verdicts/A2.4-security.json: PASS, P2 S1 (the unapproved what-if uses the full subscription-scope ARM connection)
+- docs/qa/build-F5.md observation O1: located the defect at stage start and named a what-if-only stage
+- docs/trace/build/F5/loop.jsonl gate.qa (925fe90): restated O1 at job level ("inside the deployment job")
+- docs/trace/build/F5/activity.jsonl: the red and green builders edited without reading O1; both commit subjects say "in its own job"
+- infrastructure/pipelines/stage-template.yml at c2af74d: WhatIf job and the policies-<env> deployment job in one stage, Deploy_<env>
+- infrastructure/pipelines/check-pipeline.py: stage lookups by index (stages[0]) in four places; no stage-level assertion
+## Findings
+Yes: the cause is upstream of the builders, in how the orchestrator handed on the QA defect, and it carried into
+both the infrastructure and the checker. Findings: [findings/A2.4.md](../../trace/build/F5/findings/A2.4.md).
+- I1 (R1, new): stage-template.yml:15-56 puts the what-if job in the stage that consumes environment policies-<env>; checks run at stage start, so the prod approval precedes the what-if. The comment at :19-21 is false.
+- I2 (R2, new): check-pipeline.py:62, :76-86 assert job-level separation; :62-63, :153, :181 hard-code stages[0]; the stage schema allows a condition that could bypass the order. Red, green and the mutation proof all encoded the brief's model.
+- I3 (R3, new): docs/qa/build-F5.md cites pre-fix lines; the in-task QA route has no step that re-runs the script against the fixed head.
+- I4 (security S1, new): stage-template.yml:34 runs the unapproved what-if on the deploy connection with subscription write rights; an approval check on that connection would gate the what-if stage and bring the defect back.
+The exact brief text can't be cited: dispatch prompts are not recorded in the trail.
+## Recommendations
+```yaml recommendations
+- {kind: amend-node, target: build/F5, reason: "Two stages per environment: WhatIf_<env> (regular job, no environment) then Deploy_<env> (dependsOn WhatIf_<env>, no condition, the only stage on policies-<env>); approvals live on the environment only; fix the false comment. The brief quotes QA O1 verbatim, not a restatement", status: accepted}
+- {kind: amend-node, target: build/F5, reason: "Checker asserts the stage structure (what-if stage has no environment or deployment job; the deploying stage depends on it with no condition), finds stages and jobs by key never index, drops the redundant ordering check; mutation proofs are the c2af74d layout and a deploy stage with condition: always()", status: accepted}
+- {kind: amend-node, target: build/F5, reason: "The what-if stage gets its own lower-privilege ARM connection per environment (a custom role: Reader plus the what-if action, verified against the ARM docs) as a separate template parameter, listed with the out-of-repo prerequisites", status: rejected}
+- {kind: amend-node, target: build/F5, reason: "After the fix, the tester re-runs docs/qa/build-F5.md against the new head: every stage-template line re-cited, steps 17-18 and O1 rewritten, new commit under test", status: accepted}
+- {kind: feature, target: null, reason: "Record each agent dispatch prompt in the trail, so a brief can be audited when a FAIL traces back to it", status: rejected}
+```
+## Why
+The attempt-3 stop on build/F5: reviewer FAIL A2.4 after the in-task QA fix (red 6fe2b8d, fix c2af74d).
+## Approach
+Read-only: the F5 trail (loop, activity, verdicts A1 to A2.4, findings A1), the QA script and the two pipeline files
+at c2af74d; one investigator dispatch. Time box 30m from 07:43Z; it held (diagnosis by 07:47Z).
+## Rejected alternatives
+- An approval check on the ARM service connection instead of a separate what-if connection (security S1's second option): checks run for every protected resource a stage uses, so it would gate the what-if stage and reproduce the QA defect.
+- Recording the defect as a README known limit and moving on: David already ruled "fix now" at gate.qa, and an approver who can't see the what-if is the thing the acceptance pairing exists to prevent.
+- Another job-level fix inside one stage: the same fingerprint, which the ladder would escalate.
+## Prior work consulted
+docs/plans/active/inv-001.plan.md: rule 3 (P0/P1 close in-task; re-review P2s are carried). docs/plans/active/build.plan.md and build.dag.yaml: F5 acceptance. No lessons file entries on pipelines.
+## Attempt log
+- attempt 1 · verdicts/A1-reviewer.json FAIL · grant step's FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER had no server identity for the Graph lookup (closed by the SID route)
+- attempt 2 · verdicts/A2, A2.2, A2.3 PASS · P2s closed in-task by David's rulings
+- attempt 2 · verdicts/A2.4-reviewer.json FAIL · QA what-if fix split jobs, not stages (the orchestrator's job-level restatement of QA O1)
